  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 346 AM CDT Thu Jul 23 2026\n\nToday through next Thursday...\n\nAnother day of dangerous heat stress is in the forecast for today \nthanks to a combination of ridging aloft and increasing moisture \nfrom the surface. Afternoon highs will peak in the low to mid 90s \nacross the area. This combined with dewpoints in the 70s will allow \nfor peak heat index values around 105 degrees across much of the \narea. A few changes were made to the heat graphic for the late\nweek and the \"Limited\" risk was extended further north. Likewise,\nthe \"Elevated\" risk for dangerous heat was removed. \n\nRegarding the rain chances, the slowly meandering Tropical Storm \nBertha remains just off of the Gulf coast and continues to push west \ntowards towards southwestern Louisiana. This will allow for some \ndecent rain chances (around 30-55%) across a good portion of our \narea later this afternoon. A few strong storms cannot be ruled out. \nThe scattered convection today could have an affect on how high our \nheat index values could get. \n\nDangerous heat stress will continue through the rest of this week \nand into the weekend with heat values remaining around 105 to 110 \ndegrees through Sunday. Expanding influence from the ridge aloft \nwill likely push us back into Elevated and Significant heat stress \nby early next week and into the mid week. Additional heat headlines \nand advisories/warnings may eventually be needed as we get closer. \nHeat trends will continued to be monitored. Rain chances will begin \nto increase by next Thursday. /CR/",
